{
  "image_name": "f32fac6062fdd452fa022ef8495b9157.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure is a roadmap showing how two classes of 2D materials may progress into bioelectronic applications from about 2025 to 2040. It compares graphene-based devices in the upper half with X-enes and transition metal dichalcogenides in the lower half, placing likely application areas along a time axis.\n\nOverall, the message is that simpler, near-term wearable and sensing technologies come first, while more integrated, intelligent, and implantable systems appear later. For graphene, the path starts with consumer-ready electronic tattoos and smart mobile health tools, then moves toward bidirectional sensors and actuators, tissue-conformable biohybrids, haptic or AR/VR neurorehabilitation, and eventually long-lived brain–machine interfaces, neuromorphic biohybrids, and bioelectronic therapeutics.\n\nThe X-enes/TMDs track emphasizes flexible circuits, optoelectronics, e-skin, integrated biosensors, wearable 5G communication, neuromorphic and soft circuits, then closed-loop implants, connected mHealth IoT, and finally self-sustained bioelectronics.\n\nA legend at the bottom links these milestones to key technical hurdles that must be solved, including scalable manufacturing, environmental stability, power supply and harvesting, wireless operation, tissue integration, interconnects, electronic performance, robustness, variability, and secure intelligence. The figure therefore presents both opportunity and the engineering challenges needed to reach it.",
